ses."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you need surgery for a broken jaw?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not always. Non-displaced or minimally displaced fractures can be treated with a soft diet, pain control, and weekly review. Displaced fractures usually need surgery — either closed reduction with arch bars and elastic wires, or open reduction and internal fixation (ORIF) with titanium plates and screws."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How is a broken jaw diagnosed?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Diagnosis combines a clinical exam (palpation, bite check, intra-oral inspection, nerve test) with imaging. A CT scan of the facial bones is the gold standard and is now standard of care in most centres. Plain X-rays (orthopantomogram) may be used as a first screen."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a broken jaw a medical emergency?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. A suspected broken jaw should be assessed in an emergency department or maxillofacial unit the same day. Airway compromise, severe bleeding, and associated head or neck injury take priority. Early treatment (ideally within 24–72 hours) gives the best outcome."}}]}
